      "knowledge_point": {
        "title": "Differentiating a Sum of Functions Without Constant Factors Using the Sum Rule"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "723e7a54-cfc4-55b5-8c13-7066946d5c44",
        ":question/problem": "Find ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ for  $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{(3-1)}+(-2){x}^{(-2-1)}-1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{-3}-1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
